    "## Data Set:\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Describe the FSHD dataset used in your project. Include:\n",
    "\n",
    "For this project, we used the provided FSHD dataset, which includes information from 34 participants with measurements collected at baseline, Year 1, and Year 2. The clinical dataset has patient information such as age, sex, and D4Z4 repeat length, along with MRI measurements, muscle strength, and functional assessments. Since our research question focuses on finding whether MRI fat fraction or muscle strength is more sensitive to FSHD disease progression, these were the two main measurements used in our analysis. We also used the provided MRI image dataset to find changes in fat content within the tibialis anterior muscle.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- The subject and visit/timepoint(s) analyzed\n",
    "\n",
    "For our detailed MRI image analysis, we examined subject UW_0020 at two timepoints, TP1 and TP2. We analyzed eight MRI slices from the left tibialis anterior muscle at each timepoint to examine changes in fat fraction. For our clinical analysis, we used measurements from baseline, Year 1, and Year 2. Although the clinical dataset included 34 participants, only 21 had complete right-leg MRI fat fraction and muscle strength measurements at all three visits. We used these 21 participants to be able to compare changes in MRI fat fraction and muscle strength over time. Since the actual visit times differed between participants, Year 1 visits occurred 11.15 to 24.3 months after baseline and Year 2 visits 22.13 to 42.77 months after baseline, we used linear interpolation to estimate each participant's values at two common timepoints, 11.15 and 22.13 months.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The tibialis anterior MRI data\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "We use MRI images of the tibialis anterior (TA), a muscle in the lower leg that helps lift the foot. The MRI images allow us to examine fat content within the muscle. We use these measurements to study changes associated with FSHD.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The water-image and fat-image files\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset includes water and fat MRI images saved as .npy files. Water images contain MRI signals associated with water, while fat images contain signals associated with fat. We use both signals to calculate fat fraction.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The tibialis anterior segmentation mask\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "The segmentation mask identifies the TA muscle in each MRI image. Pixels labeled 1 represent the muscle, while pixels labeled 0 represent areas outside the muscle. This allows us to calculate fat fraction specifically within the TA muscle.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The sampled locations along the muscle\n",
    "\n",
    "The MRI dataset had eight sampled locations along the tibialis anterior muscle at each timepoint, corresponding to 10%, 20%, 30%, 40%, 50%, 60%, 70%, and 80% of the way along the muscle. The provided slice_info.csv file identifies each slice's timepoint, percentage location, and distance from the start of the muscle in millimeters. The same percentage location did not always correspond to the same physical distance at both visits. For example, the 40% slice was located at 92 mm at TP1 and 96 mm at TP2. Therefore, we used distance in millimeters rather than percentage location for our interpolation analysis. We calculated fat fraction at the available locations for TP1 and TP2 and used linear interpolation to estimate fat fraction at 100 mm along the muscle. This allowed us to compare fat fraction at the same distance from the muscle start between the two timepoints.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The meaning and units of the image signals\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "The water and fat images contain numerical MRI signal intensities. These signals are combined to calculate fat fraction, which is expressed as a percentage (%). A higher fat fraction generally indicates more fatty infiltration in the muscle.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The clinical, biomarker, or functional measurements included in the dataset\n",
    "The clinical and MRI datasets were provided through BME 2315 Module 2. The clinical dataset has participant characteristics and measurements from baseline, Year 1, and Year 2. The MRI teaching dataset contained water-signal images, fat-signal images, segmentation masks, and slice-location information for subject UW_0020 at TP1 and TP2.\n",
    "The accompanying research paper by Wong et al. (2024) explains the relationship between MRI fat infiltration, muscle characteristics, and disease-related molecular changes in FSHD. We used the sources we were given in datasets for our calculations and the research paper to support our scientific background and methodology.\n",
    "\n",
    "- The source of the data\n",
    "\n",
    "For this project, the water and fat MRI images and segmentation masks were provided as part of the course dataset. According to the README file included with the MRI data, the images represent cross-sectional samples of the left tibialis anterior muscle of subject UW_0020 at two visits. Each sample has a water-signal image, a fat-signal image, and a binary segmentation mask identifying the TA muscle.\n",
    "The images include the portion of each image section containing the TA muscle, along with some surrounding anatomy and background. The segmentation masks were already provided, so we did not create them ourselves. Instead, we used the masks to identify which pixels belonged to the TA muscle and calculated fat fraction from the water and fat signals.\n",
    "The accompanying Wong et al. (2024) paper talks about the use of MRI to evaluate regional and whole-muscle fat infiltration in FSHD. However, the provided MRI teaching files do not specify every step used to acquire the images or generate the segmentation masks. Therefore, our methodology focuses on how the provided images and masks were used to calculate and compare fat fraction.\n",
    "\n",
