   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Interpolation Analysis:\n",
    "\n",
    "For this part of our Interpolation Analysis, we used linear interpolation to estimate the fat fraction at a location along the tibialis anterior (TA) muslce where we did not have a direct sampled MRI slice. From the data set, we used the distance_from_muscle_start_mm column from slice_info.csv to identify each of the slice's location in mm. Specifically, for subject UW_0020, we analysed eight of the sampled location at both TP1 and TP2, and calculated the average fat fraction within the TA muslce, respective to it's location.\n",
    "\n",
    "We chose to estimate the fat fraction at 100mm along the muscle using np.interp(). Since 100mm fell between two measured location at both visits, we performed interpolation rather than extrapolation. At TP1, the surrounding measured locations were 92mm and 116mm, and at TP2, they were 96mm and 120mm. Linear interpolation assumes that the fat fraction changes at a constant rate between the neighboring measurements.\n",
    "\n",
    "Through this method, we estimated a fat fraction of 26.91% at TP1 and 52.40% at TP2 at 100mm. Our graph shows the measured mean fat fractions along the muscle, with diamond shaped markers representing the interpolated estimates. The results suggest that fat fraction increased between the two visits at this location, indicating increased fat replacement within the muscle.\n",
    "\n",
    "But, these interpolated values are estimates rather than new MRI measurements. The actual fat fraction at 100mm can differ because fat may not have been distributed evenly between the sampled locations. Additionally, differences in image quality, the muscle segmentation, and the limited number of sampled slices can affect the accuracy of our estimates."
   ]
  },
