   "source": [
    "## Conclusions:\n",
    "\n",
    "Explain what your analysis suggests about measuring FSHD progression.\n",
    "\n",
    "Based on our research question, “Which is more sensitive to FSHD disease progression, MRI fat fraction or muscle strength?”, our analysis suggests that MRI fat fraction may be a more sensitive measure of disease progression than muscle strength in the dataset we examined.\n",
    "\n",
    "Discuss:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Which MRI, biomarker, or functional measures changed most consistently\n",
    "\n",
    "MRI fat fraction showed more consistent evidence of change compared to muscle strength. The average MRI fat fraction increased by 5.88%, with a standard deviation of 12.38, while muscle strength increased by 4.98%, with a higher standard deviation of 20.24. MRI fat fraction also showed a statistically significant change (p = 0.0417), while muscle strength did not (p = 0.2732). These results suggest that MRI fat fraction may be better at detecting gradual changes in muscle composition, although the statistical results alone do not prove that it is more sensitive.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Which measures appeared most sensitive to differences across muscle locations or visits\n",
    "\n",
    "MRI fat fraction seems to be more sensitive to changes between visits and across muscle locations. In our analysis of the tibialis anterior muscle, the interpolated fat fraction at 100 mm increased from 26.91% at TP1 to 52.40% at TP2. This shows an increase in fatty infiltration at that location between visits. MRI also allowed us to find differences across slices of the muscle, which muscle strength testing cannot directly measure. However, these MRI results were based on one subject and should not be generalized to all patients.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Whether the measures could be useful as biomarkers or exploratory clinical-trial endpoints\n",
    "\n",
    "Our findings suggest that MRI fat fraction could be useful in FSHD clinical trials because it helps researchers measure changes in muscle fat over time. MRI may detect disease progression even when changes in muscle strength are small or hard to notice. However, muscle strength testing is also important because it shows how well patients can use their muscles in everyday activities. Using both methods together could help researchers better understand how FSHD progresses.\n",
    "\n",
    "- What additional evidence would be needed before using them to evaluate treatment effectiveness\n",
    "Before MRI fat fraction or muscle strength could be used to evaluate treatment effectiveness, more research would be needed with larger patient populations and longer follow-up periods. Researchers would also need to confirm that the measurements are reliable and reproducible across different patients, muscle groups, and testing conditions. Additionally, studies should investigate whether changes in MRI fat fraction are associated with meaningful changes in muscle strength and patients' daily functioning. Treatment studies would be necessary to establish whether these measurements can reliably detect the effects of therapy.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Potential ethical implications of selecting one measure over another, including patient relevance, accessibility, and the risk of overlooking meaningful changes in function\n",
    "\n",
    "Choosing one measurement over another raises ethical concerns related to patient accessibility, cost, and clinical relevance. MRI provides detailed information about muscle composition, but it can be expensive and may not be easily accessible to all patients. Muscle strength testing is generally more accessible and directly measures physical abilities that affect patients' daily lives. Relying only on MRI could overlook important functional changes, while relying only on muscle strength could miss early changes in muscle composition. Using both methods together could help doctors better understand how FSHD progresses and how it affects patients."
   ]
  },
