   "id": "f023b735-6efb-43ed-a03d-eb4a9cdb734e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Limitations and Future Work:\n",
    "\n",
    "Describe the limitations of your project, including limitations of the sample size, available visits, MRI measurements, segmentation, biomarkers, functional measures, and analysis methods.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you had more time or additional data, explain how you would improve the analysis. Examples include analyzing more participants, adding more timepoints, improving segmentation, comparing additional muscles, validating the MRI measure against functional outcomes, or testing whether the measure is sensitive to treatment response.\n",
    "\n",
    "A major limitation of our study was having a small sample size. Although the clinical dataset included 34 participants with FSHD, only 21 had the complete MRI fat fraction and muscle strength measurements needed for our analysis. This limits how well our findings shows the larger FSHD population. Additionally, participants were followed for approximately two years with only three visits, which may not be enough to observe the full progression of a FSHD that often grows and progresses slowly over time.\n",
    "Another limitation was that our clinical analysis focused only on the right tibialis anterior muscle, while our detailed MRI image analysis examined the left tibialis anterior muscle of a single patient, UW_0020, at two timepoints. Since FSHD can affect muscles differently and may progress unevenly between the two sides of the body, our results may not apply to other muscles or patients, and we can’t speak for other muscles that are affected by this disease. Our detailed MRI analysis also included only eight slices per visit and relied on the provided segmentation masks. Errors in identifying muscle boundaries or differences between slices could affect the calculated fat fraction values.\n",
    "Furthermore, our analysis focused only on MRI fat fraction and muscle strength, so it did not include other biomarkers or functional measurements that could provide additional information about disease progression. For future work, we would include more patients, so we can have more time to follow them over for a longer period with additional visits to better understand how FSHD progresses. We would also examine additional muscle groups to determine whether the same patterns occur throughout the body. In the future studies, we would also compare MRI fat fraction and muscle strength with other biomarkers and functional measurements to determine which methods best show the disease progression. Finally, studying patients before and after treatment could help researchers determine whether MRI fat fraction or muscle strength is more useful for detecting treatment-related changes in FSHD clinical trials.\n"
   ]
  },
